---
# For configuration options and examples, please see:
# https://gethomepage.dev/latest/configs/service-widgets

# Cluster + per-node CPU/memory, read from metrics.k8s.io through the pod's ServiceAccount
# (roles/k8s/homepage: kubernetes.yaml.j2 `mode: cluster` + rbac.yaml.j2). This template is
# shared with the archived Docker role, where it is inert: that deployment has no cluster
# identity, and homepage renders an unreachable info widget as nothing rather than erroring.
- kubernetes:
    cluster:
      show: true
      cpu: true
      memory: true
      showLabel: true
      label: cluster
    nodes:
      show: true
      cpu: true
      memory: true
      showLabel: true

# Kagi. `custom` because homepage ships no built-in kagi provider - the set is
# google/duckduckgo/bing/baidu/brave/custom. The URL carries no session token on purpose:
# the search widget is a client-side navigation, so homepage serves this URL to every
# browser that loads the dashboard and the query string lands in that browser's history.
# A Kagi session token there is full-account access held in plaintext by every viewer.
# Tokenless, Kagi answers from the browser's own session cookie and prompts for a login
# when there is none. No suggestionUrl: Kagi publishes no unauthenticated autocomplete
# endpoint, so `showSearchSuggestions` has nothing to read.
- search:
    provider: custom
    url: https://kagi.com/search?q=
    target: _blank

# Longhorn volume and node capacity. It dials `longhorn-frontend`, NOT `longhorn-backend`, and
# the distinction is the whole reason this widget works. The manager's own API is fenced by
# Longhorn's chart-owned `longhorn-manager` NetworkPolicy, which admits six same-namespace
# Longhorn components and nothing else — homepage cannot reach it, and widening that object means
# editing something the Longhorn deploy owns and would revert. `longhorn-frontend` is selected by
# NO NetworkPolicy in longhorn-system, and its nginx proxies `/v1` through to the manager as
# `app: longhorn-ui`, which that policy DOES admit. So the widget needs no NetworkPolicy change
# on either side.
#
# This is also why the node-local-manager trap (only that node's manager answers a HOST caller)
# does not apply: the caller is a pod, and it addresses the frontend Service rather than the
# backend one. Verified 2026-09-06: `GET /v1/nodes` on the frontend ClusterIP answers 200 with
# both nodes' disk conditions.
- longhorn:
    url: http://longhorn-frontend.longhorn-system.svc.cluster.local
    expanded: true
    total: true
    labels: true

- openmeteo:
    label: Current
    latitude: {{ lat }}
    longitude: {{ long }}
    timezone: {{ tz }}
    units: imperial
    cache: 5
